// MK7 §5: reading a *field* of an owned call temporary. `mkName()` returns a
// `datac` that owns a `String`, and a statement that reads only a field of the
// temporary leaves the temporary itself owned by nobody else, so it has to be
// freed once the statement that read through it is done. The backend stages the
// temporary into a scratch slot, reads the field out of it, and records the
// drop for the slot: without that drop site the `Named` the call returned
// outlived the statement and its `String` was never freed.
//
// The field read here is the `i64` one, which is what makes this fixture a
// *C-buildable* shape: the C backend emits a valid `printf` for a scalar field
// of an rvalue (it does not for an owned field; taking the address of one is
// invalid C, `heap_field_of_owned_temporary.kp`), so the fixture is in the
// valgrind gate. What it now pins is that the field of a call temporary is
// freed at the end of the statement that read it, once per statement, so
// `print(mkName().r)` and `take(mkName().r)` each free the `String` the call
// allocated. The last statement is the control: a field read of a *named*
// local is a place whose owner is the scope, not the statement, so it must
// still free nothing there.
datac Named(val r: i64, val name: String)

fun mkName() => Named = Named(r = 7, name = String.from_str("hi"))

fun take(n: i64) {
    print(n)
}

fun area(n: Named) => i64 = n.r

fun main() {
    // A field read of a call temporary, in `print` position.
    print(mkName().r)
    // The same temporary read as a call argument.
    take(mkName().r)
    // A field of a temporary passed by borrow as a whole value: the ordinary
    // borrowed-temporary path, which must keep freeing exactly once.
    print(area(mkName()))
    // A field of a *named* local, which is a place and is not a temporary,
    // the borrow stages nothing and nothing may be freed here.
    val n = Named(r = 3, name = String.from_str("bound"))
    print(n.r)
}
